INT. HOSPITAL ROOFTOP HELIPAD - NIGHT

Wind. City lights below. A windsock snaps.

DR. EMEKA BALOGUN-HARTE, 50s, scrubs under a parka, sits on a bench with a paper cup. Alone. Steam rises from the coffee.

The stairwell door clangs. A young woman steps out, hood up.

NURSE ILSE VANDERMEER, 20s, badge swinging. She sits beside him without asking.

ILSE
Quiet night.

EMEKA
Don't say that.

ILSE
Sorry.

She pulls her hands into her sleeves. They watch the city.

EMEKA
Mrs. Okoro's husband brought her flowers.

ILSE
Bed twelve.

EMEKA
Yes.

ILSE
They're pretty.

EMEKA
Tulips.

ILSE
Her favourite, he said.

He nods. Sips. Sets the cup down on the bench. Turns it a quarter turn.

The stairwell door bangs. ORDERLY DESHAWN PALMQUIST, 20s, scrubs, out of breath, leans out.

DESHAWN
Doctor, they're asking for you on twelve.

A pager chirps. Neither Emeka nor Ilse moves.

ILSE (CONT'D)
That's yours.

EMEKA
I know.

ILSE
Aren't you going to--

EMEKA
In a minute.

The pager chirps again. Louder. He silences it. Folds his hands.

ILSE
Doctor?

EMEKA
Twenty-two years. I still can't tell them on a bad night. I wait for a good one.

ILSE
Tell them what?

He doesn't answer. Looks at her. Then at the stairwell door.

ILSE (CONT'D)
Doctor Balogun-Harte. What did the scan show?

EMEKA
Not on the rooftop.

ILSE
Is it her?

EMEKA
Ilse.

ILSE
Is it Mrs. Okoro?

He stands. Picks up the cup. Drops it into the bin without drinking. The coffee splashes the rim.

EMEKA
She asked me this afternoon whether she could go home Friday.

ILSE
And you said?

EMEKA
I said we would see.

ILSE
(a beat)
You said "we would see."

EMEKA
I said it very kindly.

Ilse stands too. Her hands emerge from her sleeves, one tucking a lock of hair behind her ear, the other gripping the bench rail.

ILSE
Does the husband know?

EMEKA
Not yet.

ILSE
Those tulips.

EMEKA
I know.

The windsock snaps. The wind rises. Down in the city a siren begins and fades.

He opens the stairwell door and holds it for her. She walks through without a word. He follows. The door closes on the rooftop, the bench, the bin with the spilled coffee.
